    <h3 id="Abstract">Abstract</h3>
    <pre>
        With the rapid growth of technology, and advancements in the oil and energy sector, there has been a clear interest
        in the investment of renewables by governments with scientist approved backing due to the growing
        mutual concern of climate change. In this article we will be mainly focusing on one specific application
        which generally reflects the pros and cons of most of all sources of renewables.
    </pre>

    <h3 id="Introduction">Introduction</h3>
 
        There are different types of hydrogen, the main ones being Green, Grey and Blue, we will be mainly focusing on Green,
        it is produced by electrolysis from renewable sources involving breaking down water molecules (H2O) into oxygen (O2) 
        and hydrogen (H2) <a href="https://www.iberdrola.com/sustainability/green-hydrogen">(Iberdrola, 2022)</a>. This is the general 
        overview, but to delve further, to first obtain Hydrogen there are three main steps: Molecular Transformation; Gasification 
        and Water Electrolysis. In the initial stages, a high temperature water vapour is used to separate the carbon from the 
        hydrogen that makes up the natural gas <a href="www.acciona.com">(www.acciona.com, n.d.)</a> to obtain dihydrogen and carbon dioxide. 
        Then in Gasification, the biomass is burned in a reactor at high temperatures <a href="https://www.energy.gov/eere/fuelcells/hydrogen-production-biomass-gasification">(US Department of Energy, 2019)</a>
        to make a desired yield. Finally, the products undergo water electrolysis to separate the hydrogen completely 
        with the help of direct electrical current which connects to the anode and cathode via the water.
    </pre>

    <h3 id="AnalysisAndDiscussion">Analysis and Discussion</h3>
 
        There are different types of hydrogen, the main ones being Green, Grey and Blue, we will be mainly focusing on Green, it is produced
        by electrolysis from renewable sources, which involves breaking down water molecules (H2O) into oxygen (O2) and hydrogen (H2) 
        <a href="https://www.iberdrola.com/sustainability/green-hydrogen">(Iberdrola, 2022)</a>. This is the general overview, but to delve further, 
        to first obtain Hydrogen there are three main steps: Molecular Transformation; Gasification and Water Electrolysis. In the initial stages 
        a high temperature water vapour is used to separate the carbon from the hydrogen that makes up the natural gas to obtain dihydrogen 
        and carbon dioxide. Then in Gasification, the biomass is burned in a reactor at high temperatures <a href="https://www.energy.gov/eere/fuelcells/hydrogen-production-biomass-gasification">(US Department of Energy, 2019)</a> to make a desired yield. 
        Finally, the products undergo water electrolysis to separate the hydrogen completely with the help of direct electrical current which 
        connects to the anode and cathode via the water.
    </pre>

